<main id="main-content">

<!-- â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• 00 Â· OVERVIEW â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• -->
<section class="case-hero wrap" id="s-overview">
  <div class="case-hero__crumb reveal in">
    <a href="index.html">Home</a>
    <span class="sep">/</span>
    <a href="work.html">Work</a>
    <span class="sep">/</span>
    <span>Case 02 Â· SprintMart Shared Cart</span>
  </div>

  <h1 class="reveal d-1 in">One cart.<br>The whole <em>household</em>.</h1>

  <div class="case-meta reveal d-2 in">
    <div class="case-meta__cell">
      <span class="case-meta__k">Role</span>
      <span class="case-meta__v">User research, feature research &amp; prototyping</span>
    </div>
    <div class="case-meta__cell">
      <span class="case-meta__k">Context</span>
      <span class="case-meta__v">IIT Madras mini-project Â· team of 6</span>
    </div>
    <div class="case-meta__cell">
      <span class="case-meta__k">Timeline</span>
      <span class="case-meta__v">June 2026 Â· one sprint</span>
    </div>
    <div class="case-meta__cell">
      <span class="case-meta__k">Deliverable</span>
      <span class="case-meta__v">React build Â· 30 screens Â· 19 components</span>
    </div>
  </div>


  <div class="trio reveal d-3 in">
    <span class="trio__mark" aria-hidden="true">Shared Cart</span>
    <div class="device device--l"><img src="assets/sprintmart/33-invite-joined.webp" alt="Invite flatmates, members joining the shared cart" width="640" height="1386"></div>
    <div class="device device--mid"><img src="assets/sprintmart/40-dashboard-top.webp" alt="Shared Cart dashboard, four members live in one cart" width="640" height="1386" fetchpriority="high"></div>
    <div class="device device--r"><img src="assets/sprintmart/55-payment.webp" alt="Each pays own, per-member split payment" width="640" height="1386"></div>
    <span class="trio__pill trio__pill--live" aria-hidden="true">Live Â· 4 members</span>
    <span class="trio__pill trio__pill--split" aria-hidden="true">â‚¹210 Â· split settled</span>
  </div>
  <div class="trio-cap reveal d-3 in">Fig.01 Â· <b>The Shared Cart</b> Â· join Â· live cart Â· split &amp; pay</div>
</section>

<!-- â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• OVERVIEW â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• -->
<section class="case-section wrap" id="s-summary">
  <div class="case-section__grid">
    <div class="case-section__head reveal in">
      <div class="eyebrow">Overview</div>
      <h2>What this <em>project</em> is.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="case-section__body reveal d-1 in">
      <p>SprintMart is a quick-commerce grocery app built as an IIT Madras mini-project. Our team of six each took a track; mine was how households actually order together.</p>
      <p>The result is the <b>Shared Cart</b>: one cart several people add to at the same time, with a visible budget and a payment split that settles itself. It shipped as a working React build, not a static prototype.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• PROBLEM â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• -->
<section class="case-section wrap" id="s-problem">
  <div class="case-section__grid">
    <div class="case-section__head reveal in">
      <div class="eyebrow">Problem</div>
      <h2>Watch how a flat<br>of three <em>orders</em>.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="case-section__body reveal d-1 in">
      <p>Requests drip into WhatsApp. One person compiles them from memory, orders alone and fronts the bill, then spends two days collecting â‚¹600s. Every quick-commerce app treats all of that as a single shopper.</p>
      <div class="pull">The cart, the payment, and the awareness of both live in <em>three different places</em>. I put them on one screen.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• MY ROLE â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• -->
<section class="case-section wrap" id="s-role">
  <div class="case-section__grid">
    <div class="case-section__head reveal in">
      <div class="eyebrow">My Role</div>
      <h2>What I did,<br>and what I <em>didn't</em>.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="case-section__body reveal d-1 in">
      <p><b>I did:</b> the household research track, the Shared Cart feature design end to end, and the design tokens the React build consumes as CSS variables. I also directed the AI-assisted build process for the screens in this case study.</p>
      <p><b>I didn't:</b> the store-operations track, the delivery-trust track, or the base app. Five teammates covered those, and their work isn't shown here.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• 02 Â· RESEARCH â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• -->
<section class="case-section wrap" id="s-research">
  <div class="case-section__grid">
    <div class="case-section__head reveal in">
      <div class="eyebrow">Research</div>
      <h2>What our research<br><em>told us</em>.</h2>
      <p class="def">My track was household ordering: <b>12 interviews, a 60-person survey, and five real group-order threads read line by line.</b> These four findings built the feature.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="case-section__body reveal d-1 in">
      <div class="ins-stack reveal-stagger in">
        <div class="ins-row">
          <span class="ins-row__tag">Insight 01</span>
          <span class="ins-row__stat"><span data-count="61" data-counted="1">61</span><span>%</span></span>
          <span class="ins-row__text"><b>Orders are group decisions paid by one person.</b> <span class="so">money must be visible to every member, before checkout.</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="ins-row">
          <span class="ins-row__tag">Insight 02</span>
          <span class="ins-row__stat"><span data-count="80" data-counted="1">80</span><span>%</span></span>
          <span class="ins-row__text"><b>One "default orderer" compiles everyone's items from memory.</b> <span class="so">everyone adds their own, so joining must cost nothing, not even a sign-up.</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="ins-row">
          <span class="ins-row__tag">Insight 03</span>
          <span class="ins-row__stat"><span data-count="2" data-counted="1">2</span><span>in 5</span></span>
          <span class="ins-row__text"><b>A duplicate lands weekly; 78% forget items.</b> <span class="so">guard the add moment itself with two-button checks, never cleanup after.</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="ins-row">
          <span class="ins-row__tag">Insight 04</span>
          <span class="ins-row__stat">â‚¹<span data-count="600" data-counted="1">600</span><span>Ã—3</span></span>
          <span class="ins-row__text"><b>Chasing money is social friction; being chased feels like surveillance.</b> <span class="so">settlement is structural: caps, quiet approvals, per-member payment.</span></span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pull">â€œI don't mind ordering. I hate being the <em>house bank</em>.â€<cite>Interview 07 Â· a household's default orderer</cite></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• 03 Â· THE MULTI-USER FLOW â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• -->
<section class="case-section wrap" id="s-flow">
  <div class="case-section__stackhead reveal in">
    <div class="eyebrow">Approach</div>
    <h2>The flow we designed:<br>two people, one <em>system</em>.</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="reveal d-1 in">
    <span class="flow-hint">Hover any step and the frame shows its actual screen</span>
    <div class="flow-panel">
    <div class="flow-board" id="flowBoard">
      <div class="flow-people">
      <div class="lane lane--org">
        <div class="lane__head">Organiser <span>Â· control without chasing</span></div>
        <div class="step" tabindex="0" data-screen="assets/sprintmart/30-shared-entry.webp" data-label="Organiser Â· 01"><span class="n">01</span><span class="t">Creates the cart</span></div>
        <div class="step" tabindex="0" data-screen="assets/sprintmart/31-create.webp" data-label="Organiser Â· 02"><span class="n">02</span><span class="t">Picks the payment model: Admin Pays or Each Pays</span></div>
        <div class="step" tabindex="0" data-screen="assets/sprintmart/32-invite-waiting.webp" data-label="Organiser Â· 03"><span class="n">03</span><span class="t">Shares the invite code</span></div>
        <div class="step" tabindex="0" data-screen="assets/sprintmart/43-dashboard-items.webp" data-label="Organiser Â· 06"><span class="n">06</span><span class="t">Reviews the joint cart as items arrive</span></div>
        <div class="step" tabindex="0" data-screen="assets/sprintmart/53-checkout-review.webp" data-label="Organiser Â· 10"><span class="n">10</span><span class="t">Locks the cart &amp; starts checkout</span></div>
        <div class="step step--terminal" tabindex="0" data-screen="assets/sprintmart/42-dashboard-payment.webp" data-label="Organiser Â· 13"><span class="n">13</span><span class="t">Pays the total, or per-member splits go out</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="lane lane--con">
        <div class="lane__head">Contributor <span>Â· autonomy without surveillance</span></div>
        <div class="step" tabindex="0" data-screen="assets/sprintmart/30-shared-entry.webp" data-label="Contributor Â· 04"><span class="n">04</span><span class="t">Joins with the code, no sign-up</span></div>
        <div class="step" tabindex="0" data-screen="assets/sprintmart/40-dashboard-top.webp" data-label="Contributor Â· 05"><span class="n">05</span><span class="t">Sees the live cart, members and budget</span></div>
        <div class="step" tabindex="0" data-screen="assets/sprintmart/44-add-choice.webp" data-label="Contributor Â· 07"><span class="n">07</span><span class="t">Adds items, marked common or personal</span></div>
        <div class="step step--terminal" tabindex="0" data-screen="assets/sprintmart/55-payment.webp" data-label="Contributor Â· 12"><span class="n">12</span><span class="t">Pays their own share via UPI</span></div>
        <div class="lane__end">Done here. The Organiser carries it to checkout</div>
      </div>
      </div>
      <div class="lane lane--sys">
        <div class="lane__head sys">System <span>Â· carries the friction</span></div>
        <div class="step" tabindex="0" data-screen="assets/sprintmart/61-activity.webp" data-label="System Â· 05a"><span class="n">05a</span><span class="t">Syncs the cart live; logs every change</span></div>
        <div class="step step--decision" tabindex="0" data-screen="assets/sprintmart/45-duplicate-nudge.webp" data-label="System Â· 08"><span class="n">08</span><span class="t">Catches duplicates: skip, or add anyway</span></div>
        <div class="step" tabindex="0" data-screen="assets/sprintmart/41-dashboard-budget.webp" data-label="System Â· 09"><span class="n">09</span><span class="t">Updates the household budget bar</span></div>
        <div class="step" tabindex="0" data-screen="assets/sprintmart/54-locked.webp" data-label="System Â· 11"><span class="n">11</span><span class="t">Opens a 10-minute payment window</span></div>
        <div class="step step--terminal" tabindex="0" data-screen="assets/sprintmart/58-shared-tracking.webp" data-label="System Â· 13"><span class="n">13</span><span class="t">Confirms one order, tracked by everyone</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="flow-stage" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="device">
          <img id="flowScreen" src="assets/sprintmart/40-dashboard-top.webp" alt="" width="640" height="1386" loading="lazy" style="opacity: 1; transform: scale(1);">
        </div>
        <span class="flow-stage__label" id="flowLabel"><b>The live cart</b><br>hover any step</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• 04 Â· THE DECISIONS â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• -->
<section class="case-section wrap" id="s-decisions">
  <div class="case-section__grid">
    <div class="case-section__head reveal in">
      <div class="eyebrow">Solution</div>
      <h2>Four calls I made<br>to make it <em>work</em>.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="case-section__body reveal d-1 in">
      <div class="states reveal-stagger in">
        <article class="state-card">
          <div class="state-card__head">
            <span class="state-card__step">Decision 01 Â· Insight 03</span>
            <span class="state-card__chip">Hick's Law</span>
          </div>
          <p class="state-card__cap"><b>Duplicates get two buttons.</b> Skip, or add anyway: one tap, never a menu. A contributor with thirty seconds of patience won't open one.</p>
          <div class="state-card__well"><div class="device"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/sprintmart/45-duplicate-nudge.webp" alt="Duplicate guard, skip or add anyway" width="640" height="1385"></div></div>
        </article>
        <article class="state-card">
          <div class="state-card__head">
            <span class="state-card__step">Decision 02 Â· Insight 01</span>
            <span class="state-card__chip">Public cap</span>
          </div>
          <p class="state-card__cap"><b>The budget is public.</b> One cap every member sees, editable until the cart locks, never a surprise at checkout.</p>
          <div class="state-card__well"><div class="device"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/sprintmart/50-editcaps.webp" alt="Edit budget cap screen" width="640" height="1385"></div></div>
        </article>
        <article class="state-card">
          <div class="state-card__head">
            <span class="state-card__step">Decision 03 Â· Insight 04</span>
            <span class="state-card__chip">Quiet approval</span>
          </div>
          <p class="state-card__cap"><b>Over-cap items wait, silently.</b> The Organiser approves or removes; the contributor never sees a call-out.</p>
          <div class="state-card__well"><div class="device"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/sprintmart/51-approval-pending.webp" alt="Budget approval pending" width="640" height="1385"></div></div>
        </article>
        <article class="state-card">
          <div class="state-card__head">
            <span class="state-card__step">Decision 04 Â· Insight 04</span>
            <span class="state-card__chip">Split &amp; pay</span>
          </div>
          <p class="state-card__cap"><b>Everyone pays their own share</b> in a 10-minute window. A miss at 9:59 never drops the cart. The Organiser covers the share, and the household still gets its groceries.</p>
          <div class="state-card__well"><div class="device"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/sprintmart/56-payment-paid.webp" alt="Payment confirmed, share settled" width="640" height="1385"></div></div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• RESULTS â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• -->
<section class="case-section wrap" id="s-outcome">
  <div class="case-section__grid">
    <div class="case-section__head reveal in">
      <div class="eyebrow">Results</div>
      <h2>What actually<br><em>shipped</em>.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="case-section__body reveal d-1 in">
      <p>The Shared Cart shipped as a working React build: <b>30 screens and 19 components</b>, covering the full flow from creating a cart to every member paying their share.</p>
      <p>It also works as a competitive point. Blinkit, Zepto and Instamart don't ship a shared multi-user cart. I checked all three directly while scoping the feature.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• LIMITATIONS â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• -->
<section class="case-section wrap" id="s-limits">
  <div class="case-section__grid">
    <div class="case-section__head reveal in">
      <div class="eyebrow">Limitations</div>
      <h2>What I'll claim,<br>and what I <em>won't</em>.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="case-section__body reveal d-1 in">
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="mono">Straight answer</span>
        <p>This is a mini-project. It has no real users and no usage data, and I won't pretend otherwise. Everything above describes a design and a build, not a measured outcome.</p>
      </div>
      <p><b>If it shipped, I'd measure:</b></p>
      <div class="measure-row">
        <span class="measure-chip">Duplicate-guard acceptance</span>
        <span class="measure-chip">Settlement time in the window</span>
        <span class="measure-chip">Orders with 2+ contributors</span>
        <span class="measure-chip">Repeat carts per household</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• LEARNINGS â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• -->
<section class="case-section wrap" id="s-learnings">
  <div class="case-section__grid">
    <div class="case-section__head reveal in">
      <div class="eyebrow">Learnings</div>
      <h2>What I'd do<br><em>differently</em>.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="case-section__body reveal d-1 in">
      <div class="takeaways">
        <div class="takeaway">
          <span class="takeaway__n">01</span>
          <span class="takeaway__text"><b>Design and build should move together.</b> Two screens were designed before anyone checked how they'd be wired in, and both needed reworking. Fixed during the sprint, and noted for next time.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="takeaway">
          <span class="takeaway__n">02</span>
          <span class="takeaway__text"><b>Social friction is a design problem, not a user problem.</b> People don't want to chase money, and they don't want to be chased. Once I stopped treating that as bad behaviour and started designing around it, the feature got much simpler.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• CONCLUSION â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â• -->
<section class="case-section wrap" id="s-conclusion">
  <div class="case-section__grid">
    <div class="case-section__head reveal in">
      <div class="eyebrow">Conclusion</div>
      <h2>One cart, the whole<br><em>household</em>.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="case-section__body reveal d-1 in">
      <p>The interesting part of this project wasn't the interface. It was noticing that quick commerce is built for one person while most Indian households order as a group, and then taking that seriously enough to design for it.</p>
      <p>Research pointed at the real friction, which turned out to be about money and social pressure rather than the cart itself. The design just moved that friction into the system instead of leaving it between flatmates.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

